  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Shajan Thomas vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 18Th Day Of January 2016/28Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 2255 of 2010 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 144/2008 Of The Iii Additional Sessions Court, Ernakulam S.T.1182/2002 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court-Ii, Aluva Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused: -------------------------------------- Shajan Thomas Aged 45 Years, Kanappilly House, Thuravoor Po Angamali, Ernakulam District By Advs.Sri.Santhosh Subramanian Sri.E.A.Thankappan Sri.Sherry J. Thomas Sri.S.Sreekumar(North Paravur) Smt.A.C.Anitha Respondents/Respondents/Complainant: ------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Angamaly Municipality Angamaly. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. R By Public Prosecutor Sri.V.S.Sreejith This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard Shajan Thomas vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/139126132/ 1 On 18-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P. No. 2255 of 2010 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 18th day of January, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The second accused in S.T.No.1182 of 2002 on the files of the Court of the Judicial Magistrate of First Class-II, Aluva, has filed this revision petition challenging the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence passed by the courts below under Sections 2 (ia), (j) and 7 (i) r/w Section 16 (1-A) (i) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- PW1, the Food Inspector on 26.9.2001 at 11.15 a.m, purchased three packets of tea dust, each packet having a weight of 100 gram each, from the shop of the first accused. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The report of the Public Analyst would show that the sample contained Tartarzine, sunset yellow FCF and Carmoisine and was therefore, adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P23 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample was adulterated. Even though Ext.P23 certificate would show that the sample was also misbranded, no conviction was entered into by the courts below for the said offence. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts.P1 to P35 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 and DW2 were examined and Exts.D1 to D7 were marked for the revision petitioner. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P23 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. Shajan Thomas vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/139126132/ 2 6. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P23 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. Shajan Thomas vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/139126132/ 2 6.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
  },
